 1.00, 0.38, 0.70), (0.38, 0.70, 0.38, 0.30), (0.38, 0.30, 0.62, 0.00)],\n    \")\": [(0.38, 1.00, 0.62, 0.70), (0.62, 0.70, 0.62, 0.30), (0.62, 0.30, 0.38, 0.00)],\n}\n\nTOFU = [(0.18, 0.04, 0.82, 0.04), (0.82, 0.04, 0.82, 0.96),\n        (0.82, 0.96, 0.18, 0.96), (0.18, 0.96, 0.18, 0.04)]\n\n\ndef glyph(ch):\n    \"\"\"The strokes for one character: folded to uppercase, tofu when unknown.\"\"\"\n    if ch in GLYPHS:\n        return GLYPHS[ch]\n    up = ch.upper()\n    if up in GLYPHS:\n        return GLYPHS[up]\n    return TOFU\n\n\ndef word_strokes(text):\n    \"\"\"``(letter index, start xy, end xy)`` arrays for every stroke of ``text``.\n\n    Coordinates stay in the unit letter cell; the caller decides where each\n    letter lands. A character that draws nothing (a space) contributes no rows\n    but still takes its place in the layout, which is what keeps words apart.\n    \"\"\"\n    rows = []\n    for i, ch in enumerate(str(text)):\n        for (x0, y0, x1, y1) in glyph(ch):\n            rows.append((float(i), x0, y0, x1, y1))\n    if not rows:\n        return (np.zeros(0, dtype=np.int64),\n                np.zeros((0, 2), dtype=np.float64),\n                np.zeros((0, 2), dtype=np.float64))\n    arr = np.array(rows, dtype=np.float64)\n    return arr[:, 0].astype(np.int64), arr[:, 1:3], arr[:, 3:5]\n\n\ndef letter_offsets(n, tracking):\n    \"\"\"Each letter's centre along the baseline, in cap heights, centred on zero.\n\n    Monospaced, because the typeface is: every letter takes the same pitch, so\n    an I occupies as much of the ring as a W. ``tracking`` scales that pitch --\n    1.0 is the font's own advance, 2.0 spreads the word over twice the arc.\n    \"\"\"\n    pitch = ADVANCE * float(tracking)\n    return (np.arange(n, dtype=np.float64) - (n - 1) * 0.5) * pitch, n * pitch\n\n\ndef ring_frame(aim_axis, aim_neg, up_axis, up_neg):\n    \"\"\"The ring's orthonormal frame as ``(aim, tangent, up)``.\n\n    ``up`` is the axis the letters circle around; ``aim`` is where the middle of\n    the word sits. Aim is re-squared against up, so picking the same axis for\n    both still draws a ring instead of collapsing to nothing. The tangent is\n    ``cross(up, aim)``, whose sign is what makes the text read the right way\n    round when you look down the up axis -- flip `invertUp` (or use a negative\n    radius) if you want it mirrored.\n    \"\"\"\n    eye = np.eye(3, dtype=np.float64)\n    u   = eye[AXIS[str(up_axis)]] * (-1.0 if up_neg else 1.0)\n    a   = eye[AXIS[str(aim_axis)]] * (-1.0 if aim_neg else 1.0)\n    a   = a - u * float(np.dot(a, u))\n    if float(np.dot(a, a)) < 1e-12:          # aim == up: pick any perpendicular\n        alt = eye[0] if abs(float(u[0])) < 0.9 else eye[2]\n        a   = alt - u * float(np.dot(alt, u))\n    a = a / float(np.sqrt(np.dot(a, a)))\n    return a, np.cross(u, a), u\n\n\ndef place(theta, radial, rise, aim, tangent, up):\n    \"\"\"Ring polar coordinates to world points, one row each.\n\n    ``theta`` turns about the up axis from the aim axis, ``radial`` is the\n    distance out from the centre and ``rise`` the lift along up. Every glyph\n    point goes through here, which is what welds the letters to the circle: a\n    glyph spans a fixed ANGLE, so the arc it covers grows with the radius and\n    each letter tapers towards the centre on its own.\n    \"\"\"\n    c = np.cos(theta)[:, None]\n    s = np.sin(theta)[:, None]\n    return (radial[:, None] * (c * aim[None, :] + s * tangent[None, :])\n            + rise[:, None] * up[None, :])\n\n\ndef band_faces(steps):\n    \"\"\"Quad faces for a ribbon lofted between two rings of `steps` points.\n\n    The points arrive as [outer ring, inner ring], so corner ``i`` of the\n    outer ring pairs with ``steps + i`` of the inner one and each quad spans\n    the gap between two neighbouring samples.\n    \"\"\"\n    a = np.arange(steps - 1, dtype=np.int64)\n    return (np.full(steps - 1, 4, dtype=np.int64),\n            np.stack([a, a + 1, steps + a + 1, steps + a],\n                     axis=1).reshape(-1))\n",
    "input_attrs": {
